select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 4, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.0", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.0 Overview", "body": "Fabozzi, F. J. (2021). Bond Markets, Analysis, and Strategies (10th ed.). MIT Press.\nFinancial Stability Board (2014). Reforming Major Interest Rate Benchmarks. Basel: FSB.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 4.\nMacaulay, F. R. (1938). Some Theoretical Problems Suggested by the Movements of Interest Rates, Bond Yields and Stock Prices in the United States since 1856. New York: National Bureau of Economic Research.\nTuckman, B., and Serrat, A. (2022). Fixed Income Securities: Tools for Today's Markets (4th ed.). Wiley."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.0", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nA dealer quotes a forward price. Which trade, if any, should be made?\nNorthwind shares trade at $28.00 and will pay no dividend during the next six months. Money can be borrowed or invested for six months at 5 percent per year with continuous compounding. A dealer stands ready to buy or sell Northwind shares for delivery in six months at the price shown. Set the dealer's quote, select a strategy, and examine the result. Then change the quote and find the level at which no strategy is profitable.\nOnly one quote leaves no strategy profitable. That quote is the forward price, and it was found without any forecast of the share price in six months. The remainder of the chapter extends the same argument to assets that pay income, to currencies, and to commodities."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.1", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.1", "heading": "Investment assets and consumption assets", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.1 Investment assets and consumption assets", "body": "An investment asset is an asset held for investment purposes by a significant number of market participants. Stocks, bonds, and currencies are investment assets, as are gold and silver, although the two metals also have industrial uses. A consumption asset is held primarily to be used: copper, crude oil, wheat, and natural gas are examples. The distinction governs the strength of the pricing results. For an investment asset, arbitrage determines the forward price exactly. For a consumption asset, arbitrage establishes only an upper limit, for a reason given in Section 4.7."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.1", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.1", "heading": "Short selling", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.1 Short selling", "body": "Some of the arbitrage strategies in this chapter require short selling, the sale of an asset that the seller does not own. The short seller's broker borrows the asset from another client and sells it in the market; the proceeds are credited to the short seller. At a later date the short seller buys the asset in the market and returns it, which closes the short position. The short seller profits if the price has fallen and loses if it has risen. While the position is open, the short seller must pay to the lender any income, such as dividends, that the asset would have paid, and must maintain a margin account with the broker."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.1", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.1", "heading": "Assumptions and notation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.1 Assumptions and notation", "body": "The derivations assume that, for at least some large market participants, there are no transaction costs, all trading profits are taxed at the same rate, money can be borrowed and lent at the same risk-free rate, and arbitrage opportunities are exploited as they arise. These assumptions need not hold for every participant; it is sufficient that they hold for a few large institutions, whose trading then determines prices. The following notation is used throughout.\nTable 4.1 Notation\nSymbolMeaning\n\\(T\\)Time until the delivery date, in years\n\\(S_0\\)Spot price of the underlying asset today\n\\(F_0\\)Forward or futures price today for delivery at \\(T\\)\n\\(r\\)Risk-free zero rate for maturity \\(T\\), per year, with continuous compounding\n\\(K\\)Delivery price of a forward contract entered earlier\n\\(f\\)Value today of a forward contract"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.2", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.2", "heading": "The forward price of an asset that provides no income", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.2 The forward price of an asset that provides no income", "body": "The simplest case is an investment asset that pays no income before the delivery date: a share that pays no dividend, or a zero-coupon bond. Section 1.9 established the result as an optional topic; because every later formula is a modification of it, the argument is restated here in full.\nResult 4.1 — No income\nFor an investment asset that provides no income, \\[ F_0=S_0\\,e^{rT}. \\]\nConsider the opening exercise, in which \\(S_0=28\\), \\(r=0.05\\), and \\(T=0.5\\), so that \\(S_0e^{rT}=28e^{0.025}=\\$28.71\\). Table 4.2 sets out the two arbitrage strategies. If the forward price exceeds $28.71, an arbitrageur buys the asset with borrowed money and sells it forward, a cash-and-carry arbitrage. If the forward price is below $28.71, an arbitrageur sells the asset short, invests the proceeds, and buys it forward, a reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage. Each strategy has zero cost today and a certain positive result at \\(T\\). Since neither can persist, \\(F_0=S_0e^{rT}\\).\nTable 4.2 Arbitrage when the forward price of a non-dividend-paying share is out of line (\\(S_0=28\\), \\(r=5\\%\\), \\(T=0.5\\))\nForward price = $29.50 (too high)Forward price = $28.00 (too low)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.2", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.2", "heading": "The forward price of an asset that provides no income", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.2 The forward price of an asset that provides no income", "body": "TodayBorrow $28.00. Buy one share. Enter a short forward at $29.50.Sell one share short for $28.00. Invest $28.00. Enter a long forward at $28.00.\nIn six monthsDeliver the share for $29.50. Repay \\(28e^{0.025}=\\$28.71\\).Receive \\(28e^{0.025}=\\$28.71\\). Pay $28.00 for the share and return it.\nRiskless profit\\(29.50-28.71=\\$0.79\\)\\(28.71-28.00=\\$0.71\\)\nThe second strategy does not in fact require short selling. An investor who already owns the share can sell it, invest the proceeds, and buy it back through the forward, and is better off by $0.71 than an investor who simply holds the share. Because many investors hold investment assets, the result does not depend on the availability of short sales."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.3#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.3", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.3", "heading": "Known income", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.3 Known income", "body": "Suppose that the asset will pay a known cash income before the delivery date, such as the coupon on a bond or a dividend already declared. The holder of a forward contract does not receive this income, whereas the holder of the asset does. Let \\(I\\) denote the present value of the income, discounted at the risk-free rate.\nResult 4.2 — Known income\nFor an investment asset that provides income with present value \\(I\\) during the life of the contract, \\[ F_0=(S_0-I)\\,e^{rT}. \\]\nThe argument follows that of Result 4.1 with one change. An arbitrageur who buys the asset needs to borrow only \\(S_0-I\\) until \\(T\\); the remaining \\(I\\) is borrowed until the income date and repaid from the income itself. The cost of having the asset at \\(T\\) is therefore \\((S_0-I)e^{rT}\\), and this must equal the forward price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.3#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.3", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.3", "heading": "Known income", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.3 Known income", "body": "Worked example 4.1 — Forward on a coupon bond\nA bond is priced at $950. It will pay a coupon of $30 in four months. The risk-free rate is 4 percent per year with continuous compounding for all maturities. Find the nine-month forward price.\n1\nPresent value of the income. \\(I=30e^{-0.04\\times4/12}=30\\times0.986755=\\$29.60\\).\n2\nForward price. \\(F_0=(950-29.60)e^{0.04\\times0.75}=920.40\\times1.030455=\\$948.43\\).\n3\nInterpretation. The forward price is below the spot price. The buyer under the forward pays later, which raises the price by the interest factor, but forgoes the coupon, which lowers it; here the second effect is the larger."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.4#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.4", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.4", "heading": "Known yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.4 Known yield", "body": "For some assets the income is better described as a percentage of the asset's price than as a cash amount. Let \\(q\\) denote the average yield per year on the asset during the life of the contract, expressed with continuous compounding. A yield of \\(q\\) means that income is paid continuously at the rate \\(q\\) multiplied by the current price, so that one unit of the asset, with all income reinvested in the asset, grows to \\(e^{qT}\\) units by time \\(T\\).\nResult 4.3 — Known yield\nFor an investment asset that provides a yield at rate \\(q\\), \\[ F_0=S_0\\,e^{(r-q)T}. \\]\nTo obtain one unit of the asset at \\(T\\) it is sufficient to buy \\(e^{-qT}\\) units today, at a cost of \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\), and reinvest the income. Financing this purchase costs \\(S_0e^{-qT}e^{rT}\\) at \\(T\\), which must equal the forward price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.5#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.5", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.5", "heading": "The value of a forward contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.5 The value of a forward contract", "body": "The forward price and the value of a forward contract are different quantities. When a contract is first entered, its delivery price \\(K\\) is set equal to the forward price, and its value is zero. Thereafter \\(K\\) is fixed by the contract, while the forward price \\(F_0\\) for the same delivery date changes with the market, and the contract acquires a positive or negative value.\nResult 4.4 — Value of a forward contract\nThe value of a long forward contract with delivery price \\(K\\) is \\[ f=(F_0-K)\\,e^{-rT}, \\] and the value of the corresponding short contract is \\((K-F_0)e^{-rT}\\).\nThe holder of the long contract can enter a new short forward at today's forward price \\(F_0\\) for the same date. The two contracts together oblige the holder to buy at \\(K\\) and sell at \\(F_0\\) on the delivery date, a certain cash flow of \\(F_0-K\\) at \\(T\\), whose value today is \\((F_0-K)e^{-rT}\\). Since the new contract costs nothing, this is the value of the original one. Substituting the appropriate expression for \\(F_0\\) gives \\(f=S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\) for an asset with no income, \\(f=S_0-I-Ke^{-rT}\\) for known income, and \\(f=S_0e^{-qT}-Ke^{-rT}\\) for a known yield."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.5#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.5", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.5", "heading": "The value of a forward contract", "kind": "example", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.5 The value of a forward contract", "body": "Worked example 4.2 — Valuing an existing forward\nSome months ago an investor entered a long forward to buy 10,000 Northwind shares at $27.00. The contract now has six months to run. The share price is $28.00 and the risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find the value of the contract.\n1\nCurrent forward price. \\(F_0=28e^{0.05\\times0.5}=\\$28.7088\\).\n2\nValue per share. \\(f=(28.7088-27.00)e^{-0.025}=1.7088\\times0.975310=\\$1.6666\\). The same figure follows from \\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}=28-27\\times0.975310\\).\n3\nValue of the contract. \\(1.6666\\times10{,}000=\\$16{,}666\\). The counterparty holds a short contract worth −$16,666."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.5#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.5", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.5", "heading": "The value of a forward contract", "kind": "warning", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.5 The value of a forward contract", "body": "Common error\nThe value of a forward is not the difference between the spot price and the delivery price. In Worked example 4.2, \\(S_0-K=\\$1.00\\), whereas the contract is worth $1.67 per share. The delivery price is paid in six months and must be discounted; equivalently, the comparison is between the delivery price and the forward price, not the spot price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.5#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.5", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.5", "heading": "The value of a forward contract", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.5 The value of a forward contract", "body": "Concept check 4.1\nA one-year forward contract on a share that pays no dividend is entered when the share price is $40 and the risk-free rate is 6 percent with continuous compounding. Which statement is correct at the moment the contract is entered?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.6#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.6", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.6", "heading": "Stock index futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.6 Stock index futures", "body": "A stock index measures the value of a specified portfolio of shares. A futures contract on an index can be regarded as a contract on that portfolio, and the dividends paid by the constituent shares can be treated, to a good approximation, as a known yield \\(q\\) on the value of the index. Result 4.3 then gives the futures price as \\(F_0=S_0e^{(r-q)T}\\), where \\(S_0\\) is the current level of the index.\nIf an index stands at 4,800, the risk-free rate is 4.5 percent, and the dividend yield is 1.5 percent, the six-month futures price is \\(4{,}800e^{(0.045-0.015)\\times0.5}=4{,}800e^{0.015}=4{,}872.54\\). If the futures were quoted at 4,890.00, an arbitrageur would sell futures and buy the shares of the index, securing 17.46 index points per unit. In the opposite case the arbitrageur would buy futures and sell the shares. This activity is index arbitrage. It is carried out by computer, through simultaneous orders for all the shares in the index, and it keeps the futures price close to its theoretical value except when markets are severely disrupted."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.6#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.6", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.6", "heading": "Forward and futures contracts on currencies", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.6 Forward and futures contracts on currencies", "body": "Consider a foreign currency from the standpoint of a domestic investor, and let \\(S_0\\) and \\(F_0\\) be the spot and forward prices of one unit of the foreign currency in units of the domestic currency. A holding of foreign currency can be invested at the foreign risk-free rate \\(r_f\\). A currency is therefore an asset that provides a known yield equal to \\(r_f\\), and Result 4.3 applies with \\(q=r_f\\).\nResult 4.5 — Currencies (covered interest parity)\n\\[ F_0=S_0\\,e^{(r-r_f)T}. \\]\nThis relation is covered interest parity. It implies that the currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward discount: its forward price is below its spot price. The discount exactly offsets the interest advantage, so that an investor cannot gain by converting money into the higher-yielding currency, investing it, and selling the proceeds forward."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.6#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.6", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.6", "heading": "Forward and futures contracts on currencies", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.6 Forward and futures contracts on currencies", "body": "Worked example 4.3 — Covered interest arbitrage\nThe spot exchange rate is $1.1000 per euro. One-year risk-free rates are 4.5 percent in dollars and 2.5 percent in euros, with continuous compounding. (a) Find the one-year forward rate. (b) A bank quotes a one-year forward rate of $1.1400. Describe the arbitrage for a trader who can borrow $1,100,000.\n1\n(a) \\(F_0=1.1000e^{(0.045-0.025)\\times1}=1.1000\\times1.020201=\\$1.1222\\) per euro. The euro, which has the lower interest rate, trades at a forward premium.\n2\n(b) Today. The quoted forward price of the euro is too high, so euros should be bought spot and sold forward. Borrow $1,100,000 at 4.5 percent; convert to €1,000,000; invest at 2.5 percent, which will produce \\(1{,}000{,}000e^{0.025}=\\)€1,025,315; sell €1,025,315 forward at $1.1400.\n3\nIn one year. Deliver the euros and receive \\(1{,}025{,}315\\times1.1400=\\$1{,}168{,}859\\). Repay \\(1{,}100{,}000e^{0.045}=\\$1{,}150{,}631\\).\n4\nRiskless profit. \\(1{,}168{,}859-1{,}150{,}631=\\$18{,}228\\), with no investment and no exposure to the exchange rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "Storage costs", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 Storage costs", "body": "Holding a physical commodity requires storage and insurance. Storage costs may be regarded as negative income. If \\(U\\) is the present value of all storage costs during the life of the contract, net of any income, Result 4.2 becomes \\(F_0=(S_0+U)e^{rT}\\). If instead the storage costs are proportional to the price of the commodity at a rate \\(u\\) per year, Result 4.3 becomes \\(F_0=S_0e^{(r+u)T}\\). These relations hold for commodities that are investment assets, such as gold and silver."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "body": "For a consumption commodity the argument is incomplete. If the forward price exceeds \\(S_0e^{(r+u)T}\\), the cash-and-carry strategy remains available: buy the commodity, store it, and sell it forward. That strategy limits the forward price from above. If the forward price is below \\(S_0e^{(r+u)T}\\), the reverse strategy requires holders of the commodity to sell it and buy it forward. Firms that hold copper or crude oil in order to use it in production are reluctant to do so, since a forward contract cannot be fed into a smelter or a refinery. The lower limit is therefore not enforced, and all that can be asserted is\n\\[ F_0\\le S_0\\,e^{(r+u)T}. \\]\nThe benefit of holding the physical commodity in preference to a forward contract — the ability to maintain production and to profit from temporary local shortages — is summarized in the convenience yield, denoted \\(y\\) and defined as the rate that converts the inequality into an equality:\n\\[ F_0=S_0\\,e^{(r+u-y)T}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "body": "The convenience yield reflects the market's assessment of the future availability of the commodity. When inventories are low and shortages are likely, the convenience yield is high; when inventories are ample, it is close to zero. For an investment asset it is zero, since otherwise arbitrage would be possible."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "kind": "example", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 Consumption commodities and the convenience yield", "body": "Worked example 4.4 — The convenience yield of copper\nCopper trades at $4.00 per pound and the six-month forward price is $4.10, the price at which Northwind hedged in Chapter 1. The risk-free rate is 5 percent and storage costs are 2 percent of the price per year. Find the convenience yield implied by the forward price.\n1\nUpper limit. \\(S_0e^{(r+u)T}=4.00e^{(0.05+0.02)\\times0.5}=4.00\\times1.035620=\\$4.1425\\). The observed forward price, $4.10, is below this limit.\n2\nSolve for \\(y\\). \\(4.10=4.00e^{(0.07-y)\\times0.5}\\) gives \\(0.07-y=2\\ln(4.10/4.00)=2\\times0.024693=0.049385\\), so \\(y=0.0206\\), or 2.06 percent per year.\n3\nInterpretation. Users of copper value immediate possession at about 2 percent of its price per year. No arbitrage is available at $4.10: buying copper and selling it forward would cost $4.1425 to deliver, more than the $4.10 received."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "The cost of carry", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 The cost of carry", "body": "The results of this chapter can be stated as one. The cost of carry, \\(c\\), is the interest paid to finance the asset, plus the storage cost, less the income earned on the asset: \\(c=r\\) for a share paying no dividend, \\(c=r-q\\) for a stock index, \\(c=r-r_f\\) for a currency, and \\(c=r+u\\) for a stored commodity. Then\n\\[ F_0=S_0\\,e^{cT}\\ \\text{ for an investment asset,}\\qquad F_0=S_0\\,e^{(c-y)T}\\ \\text{ for a consumption asset.} \\]\nA market in which forward prices increase with maturity is said to be in contango; a market in which they decrease with maturity is in backwardation. The formula shows the condition for each: contango when the cost of carry exceeds the convenience yield, backwardation when the convenience yield exceeds the cost of carry. Markets for industrial commodities move between the two as inventories change."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "The cost of carry", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 The cost of carry", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 4.1 — The cost of carry and the forward curve\nContango and backwardation\nSet the four rates. The figure shows the forward price at each maturity for an asset with a spot price of $100. Reproduce a stock index (income yield positive, no storage cost or convenience yield), gold (storage cost only), and a commodity in short supply (high convenience yield)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "The cost of carry", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 The cost of carry", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 4.2 — Three-dimensional view\nThe forward price as a function of maturity and net carry\nThe surface shows \\(F_0=100\\,e^{(c-y)T}\\). Along the line of zero net carry the forward price equals the spot price at every maturity. On one side of that line the surface rises with maturity (contango); on the other it falls (backwardation). Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.7#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.7", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.7", "heading": "The cost of carry", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.7 The cost of carry", "body": "Concept check 4.2\nCrude oil trades at $75.00 per barrel. The one-year futures price is $71.50. The risk-free rate is 4 percent and storage costs are 3 percent per year. What can be concluded?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.8#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.8", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.8", "heading": "Forward prices and futures prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.8 Forward prices and futures prices", "body": "The results of this chapter were derived for forward contracts. Section 2.7 noted that, when interest rates are constant, the futures price for a given delivery date equals the forward price, and that for contracts lasting a few months the difference caused by uncertain interest rates is small. The formulas of this chapter are therefore applied to futures prices as well, and the symbol \\(F_0\\) denotes either. One qualification concerns delivery. A futures contract usually permits delivery on any day within a period, at the choice of the party with the short position. If the cost of carry exceeds the convenience yield, that party gains by delivering as early as possible, and \\(T\\) should be measured to the beginning of the delivery period; in the opposite case, to its end."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.9#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.9", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.9", "heading": "Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.9 Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "body": "It is often supposed that the futures price is the market's forecast of the spot price at maturity. The supposition is not correct in general, and the relation between the two depends on risk.\nConsider a speculator who takes a long futures position and, at the same time, invests the present value of the futures price, \\(F_0e^{-rT}\\), at the risk-free rate. At maturity the investment provides \\(F_0\\), which is used to buy the asset under the futures contract; the asset is then worth \\(S_T\\). The speculator has in effect paid \\(F_0e^{-rT}\\) today for an uncertain amount \\(S_T\\) at \\(T\\). Let \\(k\\) be the rate of return that investors require on an investment with the risk of \\(S_T\\). The present value of \\(S_T\\) is then \\(E(S_T)e^{-kT}\\), where \\(E\\) denotes the expected value, and since the futures position costs nothing to enter, the two amounts must be equal:\n\\[ F_0\\,e^{-rT}=E(S_T)\\,e^{-kT}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad F_0=E(S_T)\\,e^{(r-k)T}. \\]\nThe required return \\(k\\) depends on the systematic risk of the asset, the part of its risk that is correlated with the market as a whole and cannot be removed by diversification. Three cases arise."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.9#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.9", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.9", "heading": "Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.9 Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "body": "Table 4.3 The futures price and the expected future spot price\nSystematic risk of the assetRequired returnFutures priceTerm\nNone\\(k=r\\)\\(F_0=E(S_T)\\)—\nPositive\\(k>r\\)\\(F_0<E(S_T)\\)Normal backwardation\nNegative\\(k<r\\)\\(F_0>E(S_T)\\)Normal contango\nA stock index has positive systematic risk, so the futures price of an index understates its expected future level. As a numerical illustration, if the expected price of an asset in six months is $4.30, \\(r=5\\) percent, and \\(k=9\\) percent, the futures price is \\(4.30e^{(0.05-0.09)\\times0.5}=4.30e^{-0.02}=\\$4.21\\). The terms normal backwardation and normal contango refer to the relation between the futures price and the expected future spot price, and were introduced by John Maynard Keynes and John Hicks, who argued that hedgers pay speculators a premium for bearing risk. They must be distinguished from backwardation and contango as defined in Section 4.7, which compare the futures price with the current spot price and can be observed directly.\nConcept check 4.3\nThe futures price of a stock index for delivery in one year is above the current level of the index. Which inference is valid?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.9#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.9", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.9", "heading": "Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "kind": "notes", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.9 Futures prices and the expected future spot price", "body": "Historical note\nThe theory of storage, which explains the difference between futures and spot prices of commodities by interest, storage costs, and the convenience yield, was developed by Nicholas Kaldor in 1939 and Holbrook Working in 1949. Working's studies of the wheat market showed that the relation between prices for different delivery months depended closely on the level of inventories.\nOn 20 April 2020 the May futures contract for West Texas Intermediate crude oil settled at −$37.63 per barrel, the first negative settlement price in the history of the contract. Demand had collapsed, storage at the delivery point in Cushing, Oklahoma, was nearly full, and holders of long positions who could not take delivery paid others to assume the obligation. The episode is an extreme instance of the principle of Section 4.7: the cost of storage, which is ordinarily a small element of the futures price, became the dominant one when capacity was exhausted."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.summary#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.summary", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.summary Summary", "body": "Table 4.4 Forward prices and the value of a long forward contract with delivery price \\(K\\)\nAssetForward or futures priceValue of long forward\nNo income\\(S_0e^{rT}\\)\\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nKnown income, present value \\(I\\)\\((S_0-I)e^{rT}\\)\\(S_0-I-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nKnown yield \\(q\\) (stock index)\\(S_0e^{(r-q)T}\\)\\(S_0e^{-qT}-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nCurrency, foreign rate \\(r_f\\)\\(S_0e^{(r-r_f)T}\\)\\(S_0e^{-r_fT}-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nInvestment commodity, storage \\(u\\)\\(S_0e^{(r+u)T}\\)\\(S_0e^{uT}-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nConsumption commodity\\(S_0e^{(r+u-y)T}\\)\\(S_0e^{(u-y)T}-Ke^{-rT}\\)\nEach formula states that the forward price equals the cost of acquiring the asset today and carrying it to the delivery date. No forecast of the future spot price enters.\nIn every case the value of a long forward is \\((F_0-K)e^{-rT}\\): zero at inception, and positive or negative thereafter as the forward price moves away from the delivery price.\nFor consumption commodities arbitrage sets only an upper limit; the convenience yield measures the benefit of physical possession and accounts for backwardation.\nThe futures price is below the expected future spot price when the asset has positive systematic risk."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.exercises Exercises", "body": "4.1 A share that pays no dividend trades at $65. The risk-free rate is 4 percent with continuous compounding. (a) Find the nine-month forward price. (b) If the forward is quoted at $66.00, describe the arbitrage and compute the profit per share.\n(a) \\(65e^{0.04\\times0.75}=65\\times1.030455=\\$66.98\\). (b) The quote is too low. Sell the share short (or sell a share already held), invest $65.00 for nine months, and enter a long forward at $66.00. At maturity the investment is worth $66.98; pay $66.00 for the share and return it. Profit: $0.98 per share.\n4.2 A share trades at $50 and will pay a dividend of $0.75 in two months and again in five months. The risk-free rate is 6 percent for all maturities. Find the six-month forward price.\n\\(I=0.75e^{-0.06\\times2/12}+0.75e^{-0.06\\times5/12}=0.7425+0.7315=1.4740\\). \\(F_0=(50-1.4740)e^{0.03}=48.5260\\times1.030455=\\$50.00\\). The interest cost and the dividends forgone happen to offset almost exactly.\n4.3 A stock index stands at 6,200. The risk-free rate is 4.2 percent and the dividend yield 1.4 percent. Find the futures price for a contract maturing in three months.\n\\(6{,}200e^{(0.042-0.014)\\times0.25}=6{,}200e^{0.007}=6{,}243.55\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.exercises Exercises", "body": "4.4 A long forward contract to buy an asset that pays no income at $120 has ten months to run. The asset trades at $116 and the risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find the value of the contract to the long party.\n\\(f=S_0-Ke^{-rT}=116-120e^{-0.05\\times10/12}=116-120\\times0.959189=116-115.10=\\$0.90\\). Although the spot price is below the delivery price, the contract has positive value because the forward price, \\(116e^{0.041667}=\\$120.94\\), is above it.\n4.5 The spot exchange rate is 150.00 yen per dollar. One-year risk-free rates are 0.8 percent in yen and 4.4 percent in dollars. From the standpoint of a Japanese investor, for whom the dollar is the foreign currency, find the one-year forward price of the dollar in yen. Which currency is at a forward discount?\n\\(F_0=150.00e^{(0.008-0.044)\\times1}=150.00\\times0.964640=144.70\\) yen per dollar. The dollar, which has the higher interest rate, is at a forward discount.\n4.6 Gold trades at $2,600 per ounce. Storage costs $12 per ounce per year, payable at the end of the year. The risk-free rate is 4.5 percent. Find the one-year futures price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:4.exercises", "chapter": 4, "section": "4.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 4, Pricing Forwards and Futures — 4.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(U=12e^{-0.045}=11.47\\). \\(F_0=(2{,}600+11.47)e^{0.045}=2{,}611.47\\times1.046028=\\$2{,}731.67\\). Equivalently, \\(2{,}600e^{0.045}+12\\).\n4.7 Wheat trades at $6.00 per bushel and the nine-month futures price is $5.85. The risk-free rate is 5 percent and storage costs are 4 percent per year. Find the convenience yield and state whether the market is in contango or backwardation.\n\\(5.85=6.00e^{(0.09-y)\\times0.75}\\) gives \\(0.09-y=\\ln(0.975)/0.75=-0.033757\\), so \\(y=12.4\\%\\). The futures price is below the spot price: backwardation.\n4.8 The expected price of an asset in one year is $84. The risk-free rate is 4 percent and investors require a return of 10 percent on the asset. (a) Find the one-year futures price. (b) What is the expected profit of a speculator who takes a long futures position on one unit?\n(a) \\(F_0=84e^{(0.04-0.10)\\times1}=84\\times0.941765=\\$79.11\\). (b) \\(E(S_T)-F_0=84-79.11=\\$4.89\\). The expected profit is compensation for bearing the systematic risk of the asset; this is normal backwardation."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
